---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import SelectBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/SelectBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="select-base-select">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> has always been the control you couldn't style.
      The dropdown belongs to the operating system, options hold only text and nothing
      animates.
    </p>
    <p>
      The usual fix is a rebuilt listbox: a button, a popup, ARIA roles and
      JavaScript for focus, keyboard and typeahead. Not anymore.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>appearance: base-select</code> on the select and on <code
          >::picker(select)</code
        > opts in to the stylable version
      </li>
      <li>
        A <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> inside the select is the trigger, and <code
          >&lt;selectedcontent&gt;</code
        > in it mirrors the chosen option
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>::picker-icon</code> is the arrow, and <code>:open</code> matches while
        the picker shows, so the arrow flips
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>::picker(select)</code> is a top-layer popover: <code
          >@starting-style</code
        > and <code>allow-discrete</code> on <code>display</code> and <code
          >overlay</code
        > animate it in and out
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`select:has(button),
::picker(select) {
  appearance: base-select;
}

::picker(select) {
  opacity: 1;
  scale: 1;
  transition:
    display 0.2s allow-discrete,
    opacity 0.2s,
    overlay 0.2s allow-discrete,
    scale 0.2s;

  @starting-style {
    opacity: 0;
    scale: 0.9;
  }
}

select:not(:open)::picker(select) {
  opacity: 0;
  scale: 0.9;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <SelectBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Older parsers drop the <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> and wrapper elements inside
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> and keep the options: a native select with the
        same value
      </li>
      <li>
        With no button left, <code>:not(:has(button))</code> matches: the select gets
        <code>appearance: none</code> and the wrapper's <code>::after</code>
        draws the same arrow
      </li>
      <li>
        A browser that parses the button but doesn't know <code
          >base-select</code
        > ignores the declaration and renders its native select
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
